    Should you buy a PS5 Pro or wait for the PS6?

The short answer is – it completely depends on how much you prioritise visual upgrades and additional storage. There's no doubt the PS5 Pro offers a great visual and performance boost to the latest games, especially as more titles release with PS5 Pro-specific enhancements, and the more than doubled storage capacity compared with the base PS5 is incredibly welcome. It's the most powerful and, quite frankly, best home console you can buy right now.

Although Sony has said new hardware is “top of mind”, not a lot of gamers appear to want the PS6 right now, primarily because they feel this generation still has a lot more to give in terms of quality games, while any brand-new hardware is likely to be very expensive due to the continuously ballooning prices of memory, storage and other crucial components used in console manufacturing.

That's why console prices have been rising so sharply and, even if the PS6 did release in the next couple of years, it could launch around the four-figure mark (although that is purely speculative). Having said that, a new PS5 Pro isn't exactly cheap either.

Giving a final verdict is quite difficult. On the one hand, because of the pricing, I don't think I would have naturally upgraded from my base PS5, as I would have made a significant loss trading it in compared with what I paid for it, and then would’ve had to put that money towards a PS5 Pro. It would have felt like buying another PS5 while only having one console to show for it. But the upgrades I've enjoyed with the PS5 Pro have been superb and they do make a noticeable difference.

My feeling is that if you already own a base PS5 and paid a lot of money for it, unless you really want that bump in visuals or expanded storage, it's probably worth sticking with what you've got until the PS6 arrives (whenever that is).

But if you don't yet own a PS5 and are looking at getting one, then, if you can afford it, the PS5 Pro is the model to go for. Especially with GTA 6 on the horizon.
